# kgsm-api.env — host-specific config + secrets for the kgsm-api systemd unit.
#
# Copy to /etc/kgsm-api/kgsm-api.env (root:<kgsm-user> 0640) and fill in. Every value here
# OVERRIDES the unit's Environment= defaults (the unit reads this file last).
#
# This file is what a fresh host is seeded from, by setup.sh and by the package alike, so every
# host-specific value is a COMMENTED example: a domain, a callback or a certificate path that is
# live here would be live on a host it does not describe. Uncommenting is how a host says which
# one it is. What stays uncommented is either blank or true everywhere.
#
# kgsm-api.settings.json, installed beside the binary, declares the API's whole configurable
# surface with its defaults. A variable here overrides ONE key of that file, spelling its path
# with "__". A variable naming a key the file does not declare binds to nothing, so check the
# spelling against that file (or kgsm-api/CLAUDE.md, which lists the same surface).
#
# The two boolean knobs take true/false ONLY. The older 1/0/yes/on spellings are refused with a
# startup error naming the key, rather than being silently read as false.

# ── Signing in ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing to set. This node signs nobody in: every session it accepts is minted by the cluster's auth
# anchor (kgsm-auth-anchor) and verified against the key that member publishes, and on a machine that
# is its own cluster the anchor runs beside this unit. What it needs is the cluster secret in
# /etc/kgsm/kgsm-cluster.env, which this unit loads before this file.

# ── SPA wiring ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Kestrel SERVES the SPA at / on this same origin (the deploy bundles it into wwwroot), so the
# SPA and API share ONE domain — there is no cross-origin request in prod.
# CORS allowlist, commented because the same-origin SPA above needs none — a same-origin request
# never triggers CORS. Set it only when a DIFFERENT origin must reach this API (a SPA served
# elsewhere, or another host's SPA in the multi-host registry). Unset means same-origin only, which
# is what the settings file already declares. (dev: unset = any origin.)
# Api__CorsOrigins=https://panel.example.com

# ── HTTPS / TLS, standalone only — Kestrel terminates TLS directly ────────────
# A cluster member sets none of this; see "Joining a cluster" below.
# The whole block below is commented, and the unit's Api__Urls=http://0.0.0.0:8080 applies until it
# is not: a certificate path is per-host, and Kestrel refuses to start when one names a file that is
# not there. Uncomment all four lines together — an https bind with no certificate is the same
# failure as a certificate path with no file.
#
# A host serves BOTH the SPA (at /) and the API (under /api/v1) on one public origin, with Kestrel
# terminating TLS itself.
# Cert = Let's Encrypt via certbot (the certbot.timer renews it — not a resident service):
#   sudo certbot certonly --standalone -d kgsm.thekrystalship.com   # port 80 must be free
# certbot writes the cert root-readable only, but kgsm-api runs as a NON-root user — so install
# the committed deploy/certbot-deploy-hook.sh, which copies the cert+key to a service-readable
# path (/etc/kgsm-api/tls, 0640) and restarts the unit on every issuance/renewal:
#   sudo install -m 0755 deploy/certbot-deploy-hook.sh \
#        /etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/kgsm-api.sh
# Then point Kestrel at the service-readable copies (Kestrel__* are STANDARD ASP.NET keys).
# Bind PUBLIC https on :443 (Kestrel terminates TLS) + a PUBLIC plain-http :80 whose ONLY job is to
# 308-redirect bare http -> https (Startup; "we don't allow bare http") + a LOOPBACK plain-http
# ops/health port (:8097, EXEMPT from that redirect — the deploy /health probe + local ops use it).
# :80 and :443 are privileged ports, so the unit grants CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE to the non-root service.
# Api__Urls=http://0.0.0.0:80;https://0.0.0.0:443;http://127.0.0.1:8097
# Kestrel__Certificates__Default__Path=/etc/kgsm-api/tls/fullchain.pem
# Kestrel__Certificates__Default__KeyPath=/etc/kgsm-api/tls/privkey.pem
# Stable base for the absolute URLs the API emits (e.g. library cover-art links), and the address
# this node offers peers when it has one to offer. With real TLS the request scheme is already https,
# but pinning a single fixed host is tidy:
# Api__PublicBaseUrl=https://panel.example.com

# ── Joining a cluster ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The secret is NOT set here. It lives in /etc/kgsm/kgsm-cluster.env as Cluster__Secret, which this
# unit loads before this file — one value the whole machine shares, because a machine can run more
# than one member of a cluster and they must all hold the same one.
#
# The cluster's DNS anchor names this node, issues its certificate and writes its nginx site, so a
# member sets none of the TLS block above, no public URL and no provider callback. What it sets:
#
# Loopback behind nginx, plus the private address it is first added at (a LAN or VPN address the DNS
# anchor's machine reaches; its first certificate is delivered there):
# Api__Urls=http://127.0.0.1:8097;http://10.44.0.4:8097
# Where this machine is reached from the internet — its dynamic-DNS name, with 443 forwarded here:
# Api__PublicHost=node.ddns.example.net
# The member id, stable forever (blank takes the machine name), and the panel's origin:
# Api__HostId=
# Api__CorsOrigins=https://kgsm.example.com
#
# An admin then adds it from the Control Panel's Cluster page at http://<private-address>:8097.
# Runbook: kgsm-meta/NODE-PROVISIONING.md §10·a.

# ── Optional overrides (defaults are in the unit) ─────────────────────────────
# Api__MonitorSocketPath=/run/kgsm-monitor/metrics.sock
# Api__KgsmPath=/usr/bin/kgsm

# ── The optional leaves — nothing to set ──────────────────────────────────────
# The firewall authority, the scheduler, the reactor, the Discord bot and the assistant each bind a
# fixed endpoint their own configuration already names, and each installs a config descriptor into
# /var/lib/kgsm/leaves. This API reads that directory: a leaf that is installed here is wired to,
# and one that is not is reported absent rather than perpetually down. Installing a leaf is
# therefore the whole of connecting it, and repeating its socket path below would only create
# somewhere for the two to disagree.
#
# Two reasons to write one of these anyway:
#   - a leaf that does not sit where its package puts it. Name the endpoint:
# Api__SchedulerSocketPath=/run/kgsm-scheduler/status.sock
# Api__SchedulerControlSocketPath=/run/kgsm-scheduler/control.sock
# Api__ReactorSocketPath=/run/kgsm-reactor/status.sock
# Api__BotSocketPath=/run/kgsm-bot/status.sock
# Api__FirewallSocketPath=/run/kgsm-firewall/firewall.sock
# Api__AssistantBaseUrl=http://127.0.0.1:5180
#   - a leaf installed here that this panel should leave alone. Set the key to nothing at all:
# Api__ReactorSocketPath=

# ── GC / memory tuning ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Workstation GC + ConserveMemory + RetainVM is now BAKED INTO THE BUILD (Api.csproj +
# src/Api/runtimeconfig.template.json), so you do NOT need anything here. These env vars are
# only an optional override if you want to A/B test the GC mode on a host without rebuilding:
# DOTNET_gcServer=0
# DOTNET_GCConserveMemory=9
# DOTNET_GCRetainVM=0
